  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "If an item is sold for \\(x\\) dollars, there is a loss of \\(15\\%\\) based on the cost. If, however, the same item is sold for \\(y\\) dollars, there is a profit of \\(15\\%\\) based on the cost. The ratio \\(y:x\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(23:17\\)",
    "B": "\\(17y:23\\)",
    "C": "\\(23x:17\\)",
    "D": "dependent upon the cost",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Both percentages are based on the same cost, so express both selling prices as multiples of that cost. Let the cost be \\(C>0\\) dollars. A loss subtracts \\(15\\%\\) of the cost, while a profit adds \\(15\\%\\) of the cost. Thus\n\\[\nx=(1-0.15)C=0.85C,\n\\qquad y=(1+0.15)C=1.15C.\n\\]\nThe common positive factor \\(C\\) cancels from the ratio:\n\\[\ny:x=1.15:0.85=115:85=23:17.\n\\]\nTherefore the ratio is \\(23:17\\), choice A.",
